{
"dur": 88.062,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 10.838,
"text": "Most experts said Japan should not even try to make its own cars. In 1950 the United States built millions of passenger cars. Japan built about sixteen hundred.",
"end": 11.113
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 11.113,
"vo_start": 11.388,
"vo_end": 22.627,
"text": "Toyota tried anyway. In 1957 it sent the Toyopet Crown to America. On American highways it shook, overheated and broke down. Americans laughed at it.",
"end": 22.902
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 22.902,
"vo_start": 23.177,
"vo_end": 33.577,
"text": "So Toyota changed how it worked. Make only what is needed, when it is needed, and let any worker stop the line if they see a problem. That system later became famous around the world.",
"end": 33.852
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 33.852,
"vo_start": 34.127,
"vo_end": 44.724,
"text": "In 1945 an engineer named Masaru Ibuka opened a radio repair shop in a burnt-out department store in Tokyo. The next year he started a company with a young navy officer, Akio Morita.",
"end": 44.999
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 44.999,
"vo_start": 45.274,
"vo_end": 50.794,
"text": "Their first product, an electric rice cooker, was a disaster. It burned the rice or left it raw.",
"end": 51.069
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 51.069,
"vo_start": 51.344,
"vo_end": 62.533,
"text": "In 1953 they bought the right to make transistors from an American company, even though Japan's own trade ministry thought it was a waste. By 1957 they had a radio small enough to slip into a shirt pocket.",
"end": 62.808
},
{
"i": 7,
"start": 62.808,
"vo_start": 63.083,
"vo_end": 74.682,
"text": "An American company offered to buy a hundred thousand radios, but only if they carried its brand name. Morita said no. He wanted his own brand on them. The brand was Sony.",
"end": 74.957
},
{
"i": 8,
"start": 74.957,
"vo_start": 75.232,
"vo_end": 87.112,
"text": "And in a wooden shed in 1946, a mechanic named Soichiro Honda strapped small engines onto bicycles. When the government later tried to limit which companies could make cars, Honda ignored it.",
"end": 88.062
}
],
